1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,051
Welcome back to Rashi in Brief, where each

2
00:00:04,110 --> 00:00:06,490
week we explore the Torah portion through the

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:10,646
eyes of the great medieval commentator, Rabbi Shlomo

4
00:00:10,739 --> 00:00:13,502
Yitzchaki, known as Rashi.

5
00:00:14,315 --> 00:00:17,450
This week's portion is called Eikev, found in

6
00:00:17,485 --> 00:00:21,223
the book of Deuteronomy, chapters seven through eleven.

7
00:00:21,827 --> 00:00:24,253
Let's dig into some of the fascinating insights

8
00:00:24,323 --> 00:00:27,539
Rashi offers on this rich and layered portion.

9
00:00:29,129 --> 00:00:33,170
Parshat Eikev continues Moses's great farewell address to

10
00:00:33,239 --> 00:00:35,585
the Israelites as they stand on the edge

11
00:00:35,608 --> 00:00:36,815
of the Promised Land.

12
00:00:37,512 --> 00:00:40,554
He urges them to keep God's commandments, promises

13
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:43,213
them blessings if they do, and warns of

14
00:00:43,271 --> 00:00:44,989
consequences if they don't.

15
00:00:45,941 --> 00:00:48,483
He reminds them of the miracles in Egypt,

16
00:00:48,727 --> 00:00:51,224
the manna in the wilderness, the sin of

17
00:00:51,282 --> 00:00:53,975
the golden calf, and the breaking of the

18
00:00:54,010 --> 00:00:54,811
tablets.

19
00:00:55,624 --> 00:00:57,714
He describes the goodness of the land they're

20
00:00:57,737 --> 00:01:01,243
about to enter, contrasting it with Egypt, and

21
00:01:01,325 --> 00:01:03,600
he pleads with them to love God and

22
00:01:03,693 --> 00:01:05,376
serve Him wholeheartedly.

23
00:01:06,491 --> 00:01:09,835
Throughout this portion, Rashi is drawn to puzzles

24
00:01:09,905 --> 00:01:13,434
in the Hebrew language, to apparent contradictions between

25
00:01:13,504 --> 00:01:16,557
different biblical passages, and to moments where the

26
00:01:16,615 --> 00:01:18,717
Torah seems to say more or less than

27
00:01:18,763 --> 00:01:19,738
we'd expect.

28
00:01:20,435 --> 00:01:23,430
He's especially attentive to the emotional and moral

29
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:26,937
dimensions of Moses's speech, reading between the lines

30
00:01:27,006 --> 00:01:30,872
to uncover layers of rebuke, encouragement, and theological

31
00:01:30,954 --> 00:01:31,592
depth.

32
00:01:33,171 --> 00:01:35,180
Let's start with the very first word of

33
00:01:35,226 --> 00:01:38,175
the portion, which actually gives it its name.

34
00:01:39,127 --> 00:01:43,597
The Torah says, "V'hayah eikev tishm'un," which is

35
00:01:43,667 --> 00:01:46,476
usually translated as something like, "And it shall

36
00:01:46,523 --> 00:01:48,671
come to pass, if you hearken to these

37
00:01:48,705 --> 00:01:53,071
commandments." But Rashi notices that the word eikev

38
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:55,892
literally means "heel," as in the heel of

39
00:01:55,950 --> 00:01:56,763
your foot.

40
00:01:57,460 --> 00:02:00,525
So Rashi reads the verse this way: if

41
00:02:00,583 --> 00:02:02,986
you will heed even the lighter commandments, the

42
00:02:03,044 --> 00:02:05,354
ones a person tends to trample underfoot with

43
00:02:05,389 --> 00:02:07,873
their heel, the ones we're inclined to treat

44
00:02:07,931 --> 00:02:11,112
as unimportant, then God will keep His promise

45
00:02:11,159 --> 00:02:11,751
to you.

46
00:02:12,703 --> 00:02:15,559
Right from the opening word, Rashi is telling

47
00:02:15,617 --> 00:02:17,927
us that the Torah is concerned not just

48
00:02:17,974 --> 00:02:21,074
with the big, dramatic commandments, but with the

49
00:02:21,155 --> 00:02:23,825
small, everyday ones we might be tempted to

50
00:02:23,895 --> 00:02:24,638
dismiss.

51
00:02:25,334 --> 00:02:28,225
It's a powerful reminder that faithfulness is measured

52
00:02:28,272 --> 00:02:29,305
in the details.

53
00:02:30,141 --> 00:02:33,310
A little further on, in chapter eight, Moses

54
00:02:33,357 --> 00:02:36,666
tells the Israelites to keep "kol hamitzvah," which

55
00:02:36,735 --> 00:02:41,565
literally translates as "the whole commandment." Rashi offers

56
00:02:41,658 --> 00:02:42,750
two readings.

57
00:02:43,446 --> 00:02:46,000
On the plain level, it simply means every

58
00:02:46,058 --> 00:02:47,788
commandment, all of them.

59
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:51,724
But then he shares a beautiful midrashic teaching:

60
00:02:52,537 --> 00:02:55,079
if you've begun a good deed, finish it,

61
00:02:55,323 --> 00:02:58,910
because a mitzvah, a commandment or meritorious act,

62
00:02:59,119 --> 00:03:01,615
is credited to the one who completes it.

63
00:03:02,428 --> 00:03:04,982
He brings the example of Joseph's bones.

64
00:03:05,795 --> 00:03:07,676
Moses was the one who retrieved them from

65
00:03:07,757 --> 00:03:09,940
Egypt, as it says in Exodus.

66
00:03:10,752 --> 00:03:12,970
But because Moses couldn't finish the task of

67
00:03:13,063 --> 00:03:15,420
burying them in the land of Israel, and

68
00:03:15,489 --> 00:03:18,821
the Israelites completed it, Scripture credits the burial

69
00:03:18,891 --> 00:03:20,075
to the Israelites.

70
00:03:20,772 --> 00:03:24,812
The lesson is striking: starting something good matters,

71
00:03:25,091 --> 00:03:27,157
but finishing it matters even more.

72
00:03:28,109 --> 00:03:31,987
Rashi draws this all from two little words,

73
00:03:32,579 --> 00:03:33,868
kol hamitzvah.

74
00:03:34,669 --> 00:03:36,317
One of the more vivid details in the

75
00:03:36,364 --> 00:03:39,336
portion comes when Moses recounts the forty years

76
00:03:39,382 --> 00:03:40,137
of wandering.

77
00:03:40,822 --> 00:03:43,620
He says, "Your garment did not wear out

78
00:03:43,678 --> 00:03:46,558
upon you, and your foot did not swell."

79
00:03:47,498 --> 00:03:51,109
Rashi brings a wonderful tradition to explain this.

80
00:03:51,713 --> 00:03:54,383
He says the clouds of Divine Glory, the

81
00:03:54,429 --> 00:03:57,622
miraculous clouds that accompanied Israel in the wilderness,

82
00:03:57,901 --> 00:04:00,362
would rub the dirt from their clothing and

83
00:04:00,443 --> 00:04:02,928
bleach them so they looked like new white

84
00:04:02,986 --> 00:04:03,764
garments.

85
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:07,328
And even more remarkably, as the children grew,

86
00:04:07,572 --> 00:04:09,847
their clothes grew with them, the way the

87
00:04:09,905 --> 00:04:11,612
shell of a snail grows along with the

88
00:04:11,659 --> 00:04:12,889
snail inside it.

89
00:04:13,841 --> 00:04:16,906
As for their feet not swelling, Rashi connects

90
00:04:16,964 --> 00:04:19,704
the word "batzeka" to the word for dough,

91
00:04:20,029 --> 00:04:20,841
batzek.

92
00:04:21,446 --> 00:04:23,339
He's saying their feet didn't puff up like

93
00:04:23,385 --> 00:04:25,997
rising dough, as normally happens to people who

94
00:04:26,044 --> 00:04:28,064
walk barefoot for long distances.

95
00:04:29,016 --> 00:04:32,533
These little details transform the wilderness experience from

96
00:04:32,568 --> 00:04:34,855
a story of hardship into a story of

97
00:04:34,936 --> 00:04:36,922
ongoing, intimate divine care.

98
00:04:37,897 --> 00:04:40,602
When Moses describes the Land of Israel, he

99
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:42,471
says it is "not like the land of

100
00:04:42,553 --> 00:04:45,130
Egypt." Now, you might read that as a

101
00:04:45,211 --> 00:04:47,522
compliment or as a criticism.

102
00:04:48,218 --> 00:04:50,610
Rashi raises exactly this question.

103
00:04:51,423 --> 00:04:53,687
Is Moses saying the land is better than

104
00:04:53,768 --> 00:04:55,509
Egypt, or worse?

105
00:04:56,322 --> 00:04:59,805
To answer, Rashi launches into a fascinating argument

106
00:05:00,130 --> 00:05:02,649
involving the cities of Hebron and Zoan.

107
00:05:03,346 --> 00:05:05,668
The Torah tells us elsewhere that Hebron was

108
00:05:05,714 --> 00:05:07,723
built seven years before Zoan.

109
00:05:08,837 --> 00:05:11,833
Rashi explains that Ham, the son of Noah,

110
00:05:12,042 --> 00:05:15,478
built both cities, Zoan for his son Mizraim,

111
00:05:15,652 --> 00:05:18,241
who is Egypt, and Hebron for his son

112
00:05:18,323 --> 00:05:19,019
Canaan.

113
00:05:19,832 --> 00:05:22,247
Naturally, a father builds the finer city for

114
00:05:22,282 --> 00:05:23,280
his firstborn.

115
00:05:23,791 --> 00:05:27,042
So Hebron, the worst part of Canaan, was

116
00:05:27,111 --> 00:05:29,898
still superior to Zoan, which was the finest

117
00:05:29,956 --> 00:05:32,870
part of Egypt, being the seat of royalty.

118
00:05:33,311 --> 00:05:35,749
And Egypt itself was already compared to the

119
00:05:35,819 --> 00:05:36,899
Garden of God.

120
00:05:37,502 --> 00:05:38,431
The conclusion?

121
00:05:39,128 --> 00:05:41,543
The Land of Israel far surpasses even the

122
00:05:41,624 --> 00:05:44,143
greatest land the Israelites had ever known.

123
00:05:45,095 --> 00:05:48,903
Beyond this, Rashi contrasts the agricultural reality of

124
00:05:48,961 --> 00:05:49,902
the two lands.

125
00:05:50,714 --> 00:05:54,406
Egypt required backbreaking labor, hauling water from the

126
00:05:54,453 --> 00:05:58,075
Nile by foot, irrigating by hand, with only

127
00:05:58,133 --> 00:06:00,014
the lowlands receiving water.

128
00:06:00,827 --> 00:06:02,650
But the Land of Israel drinks the rain

129
00:06:02,696 --> 00:06:03,369
of heaven.

130
00:06:04,182 --> 00:06:07,026
You can sleep soundly in your bed, Rashi

131
00:06:07,085 --> 00:06:10,033
says, and God waters both the hills and

132
00:06:10,115 --> 00:06:11,148
the valleys for you.

133
00:06:11,996 --> 00:06:14,817
In chapter eleven, the Torah uses a doubled

134
00:06:14,887 --> 00:06:20,227
verb form: "shamoa tishm'u," which literally means something

135
00:06:20,297 --> 00:06:24,732
like "hearkening, you shall hearken." Rashi reads this

136
00:06:24,813 --> 00:06:28,226
doubling as conveying a profound educational principle.

137
00:06:28,830 --> 00:06:30,909
If you listen to the old, if you

138
00:06:31,001 --> 00:06:33,927
review and revisit what you've already learned, then

139
00:06:33,962 --> 00:06:35,564
you will be able to hear the new,

140
00:06:35,738 --> 00:06:37,503
to absorb fresh knowledge.

141
00:06:38,107 --> 00:06:41,114
And conversely, the Torah later uses a similar

142
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:45,386
construction about forgetting: "if you begin to forget,

143
00:06:45,595 --> 00:06:49,438
you will end up forgetting everything." Rashi even

144
00:06:49,496 --> 00:06:52,050
cites a teaching written in a certain scroll:

145
00:06:52,863 --> 00:06:54,895
if you forget Me for one day, I

146
00:06:54,976 --> 00:06:56,729
will forget you for two days.

147
00:06:57,681 --> 00:07:00,619
Learning, in Rashi's view, is not a one-time

148
00:07:00,654 --> 00:07:01,269
event.

149
00:07:01,710 --> 00:07:04,311
It requires constant return and renewal.

150
00:07:05,007 --> 00:07:07,922
And connected to this, Rashi comments on the

151
00:07:07,968 --> 00:07:11,149
phrase "which I command you this day," saying

152
00:07:11,196 --> 00:07:13,552
the commandments should always feel as fresh and

153
00:07:13,703 --> 00:07:15,422
new as if you heard them for the

154
00:07:15,480 --> 00:07:17,012
very first time today.

155
00:07:17,825 --> 00:07:21,563
Repetition and freshness are not opposites; they sustain

156
00:07:21,621 --> 00:07:22,178
each other.

157
00:07:22,921 --> 00:07:25,650
Finally, near the end of the portion, the

158
00:07:25,731 --> 00:07:28,216
Torah commands us "to walk in all His

159
00:07:28,274 --> 00:07:30,944
ways." What does it mean to walk in

160
00:07:31,002 --> 00:07:31,908
God's ways?

161
00:07:32,790 --> 00:07:35,878
Rashi's answer is elegant and direct: He is

162
00:07:35,971 --> 00:07:38,444
merciful, so you be merciful.

163
00:07:39,141 --> 00:07:43,518
He bestows lovingkindness, so you bestow lovingkindness.

164
00:07:44,028 --> 00:07:45,933
And then the Torah says to "cleave to

165
00:07:46,002 --> 00:07:50,460
Him." Rashi asks the obvious question: how can

166
00:07:50,507 --> 00:07:52,980
a person cleave to God, who is described

167
00:07:53,026 --> 00:07:55,046
elsewhere as a consuming fire?

168
00:07:55,743 --> 00:07:59,319
His answer: attach yourself to Torah scholars and

169
00:07:59,388 --> 00:08:02,047
sages, and God will consider it as though

170
00:08:02,082 --> 00:08:03,336
you have cleaved to Him.

171
00:08:04,288 --> 00:08:07,666
In just a few words, Rashi transforms an

172
00:08:07,713 --> 00:08:12,125
abstract theological idea into a concrete, human practice.

173
00:08:12,937 --> 00:08:14,841
The way to draw close to the infinite

174
00:08:14,888 --> 00:08:19,149
is through community, through learning alongside others, through

175
00:08:19,172 --> 00:08:21,865
the relationships we build around shared pursuit of

176
00:08:21,935 --> 00:08:22,481
wisdom.

177
00:08:24,942 --> 00:08:28,100
Stepping back from these individual comments, we can

178
00:08:28,147 --> 00:08:31,142
see several threads running through Rashi's reading of

179
00:08:31,258 --> 00:08:32,373
Parshat Eikev.

180
00:08:32,813 --> 00:08:34,695
One is the theme of divine care in

181
00:08:34,741 --> 00:08:37,539
the details, whether it's the clothes that don't

182
00:08:37,597 --> 00:08:40,198
wear out, the rain that falls at night

183
00:08:40,267 --> 00:08:42,369
so as not to disturb you, or the

184
00:08:42,414 --> 00:08:45,318
commandments that seem small but matter immensely.

185
00:08:46,618 --> 00:08:49,196
Another is the theme of memory and renewal:

186
00:08:49,799 --> 00:08:53,898
the constant need to revisit, review, and re-experience

187
00:08:53,944 --> 00:08:56,184
the Torah as though hearing it for the

188
00:08:56,231 --> 00:08:57,078
first time.

189
00:08:58,193 --> 00:09:01,467
There's also a recurring tension between complacency and

190
00:09:01,548 --> 00:09:02,419
gratitude.

191
00:09:03,371 --> 00:09:06,715
Rashi warns, drawing on the Torah's own language,

192
00:09:07,098 --> 00:09:10,476
that fullness and prosperity are precisely the moments

193
00:09:10,523 --> 00:09:13,123
when people are most likely to forget God.

194
00:09:13,936 --> 00:09:17,721
And finally, there's Rashi's characteristic move of grounding

195
00:09:17,802 --> 00:09:19,927
lofty theology in human action.

196
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:23,143
Walk in God's ways by being merciful.

197
00:09:23,839 --> 00:09:26,220
Cleave to God by attaching yourself to wise

198
00:09:26,266 --> 00:09:27,021
teachers.

199
00:09:27,624 --> 00:09:30,341
Throughout it all, Rashi shows us that the

200
00:09:30,422 --> 00:09:33,801
Torah speaks not just to a generation standing

201
00:09:33,859 --> 00:09:36,146
at the border of Canaan, but to every

202
00:09:36,216 --> 00:09:38,259
reader who struggles with the daily challenge of

203
00:09:38,340 --> 00:09:42,392
paying attention, staying faithful, and finding holiness in

204
00:09:42,439 --> 00:09:43,147
the ordinary.

205
00:09:45,295 --> 00:09:46,955
Thank you so much for joining me on

206
00:09:47,036 --> 00:09:48,998
Rashi in Brief this week.

207
00:09:49,695 --> 00:09:51,912
Until next time, may we all find the

208
00:09:51,959 --> 00:09:53,770
wisdom to pay attention to what we might

209
00:09:53,828 --> 00:09:55,709
otherwise trample underfoot.

210
00:09:56,405 --> 00:09:58,286
Shabbat Shalom.

